#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Import runs (and kick Drive syncs) from a Matrix room.

    ./matrix-bot --homeserver sallings.org --room '!abc:sallings.org' \
        --user @dustin:sallings.org=dustin --user @walter:matrix.org=walter

Listens in each --room (repeatable), and in rooms it's invited to once a
room admin ✅s its question about the invite in the first room, to the
Matrix users --user maps to riders (and to anyone for !conditions and !help). Room admins (power level 100, read
from the room each time) can act for any rider and publish:

  * a .gpx file, captioned with a foil ("585"), is imported with add-run
    as the sender's run, then the reply links it. Sent without a caption,
    the bot asks for the foil; answer with one, or "-" for the rider's
    last foil from that start beach. A foil they haven't used before
    takes its full name after "new": "new Axis Spitfire 1180".
  * "!sync" runs drive-sync for the sender's rider: their shared Google
    Drive folder, an rclone remote named after them. A room admin's
    "!sync" syncs every rider, and "!sync walter" just Walter.
    The bot also syncs every rider by itself at 16:00, 18:00 and 23:00
    Hawaii time, telling rooms with "runs" on about any new runs.
  * "!last" summarizes the sender's latest run, "!last walter" Walter's.
    Imports reply with the same summary, plus any records the run set
    (bot/achievements.mjs, the web's achievements as JSON).
  * "!foil 585" changes the foil on the sender's latest run (matched like
    add-run's, or "new <full name>"), then re-exports. A room admin
    can name a rider first: "!foil walter orca 685".
  * "!publish" runs ./publish for a room admin, with
    the same words and flags: "!publish --dry-run", "!publish verify
    --fix", "!publish orphans --delete". Long lists in the output are cut
    short; the full output is in the bot's log.
  * "!conditions" (anyone) shows the latest wind at each station with
    its trend over the last hour, Kihei's tides, and each buoy's swells
    with an image of its spectrum and direction rose. Wind and tides come
    live from the weather/+ feed on --mqtt (default host mqtt) when it's
    up, on top of the lake's half-hourly wind. The bot also keeps
    live/wind.json in the bucket (each station's last 24 hours, for the
    hub's live wind cards) replaced at most every 30 seconds as readings
    arrive.
  * "!ask" takes plain English, read by a local model (--ollama,
    --ask-model; see ask.py). A question ("!ask when did I last go to
    Ulua?") is answered from the sender's own runs. A change ("!ask the 2
    runs with an unknown foil were the ART 999") is shown as the runs it
    would change, before and after, and a room admin's ✅ on that message
    applies it. A room admin can ask about another rider: "!ask walter
    ...".
  * "!help" says the above.
  * Any unambiguous start of a command works: "!c chart", "!l walter".

When the conditions/rating feed (Node-RED's 0-4 score per side) says a
run is getting good, the bot says so in the room, once per session per
side, plus once if it gets really good and once when it drops off: see
run_alerts.py. And once a day, when Kīhei is starting to look good (a
rating of LOOK or more) and the Wailea cam reads lively (Node-RED's vision
read of the frame on weather/conditions/wailea), it suggests a look at the
cam, with the frame.

The bot also pins a one-line version of !conditions in the room and edits
it in place every few minutes (Element X shows the pinned message in a
banner atop the room). Pinning needs the bot to have the power to change
m.room.pinned_events (moderator, 50, by default).

Start it from db's nix dev shell (or the flake's matrix-bot package, which
the home-manager module runs). It reads the bot account's access token
from --token-file (default ~/.config/dwp/matrix-token, mode 600) and
remembers where it got to in --state-dir, so a restart neither replays
old files nor misses ones sent while it was down. Use an unencrypted
room: it doesn't speak E2EE.

Only the Python standard library, like db/.
"""

